# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fixed-width fixtures: every byte is a contract, so Git must not rewrite any of them
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The committed fixtures under src/test/resources/fixtures are fixed-width sequential datasets,
# and the byte-equivalence gate compares module output against them byte for byte. Two default
# Git behaviours are wrong for that kind of file, and both are disabled here rather than left to
# a contributor's local configuration:
#
#   text       Left unspecified, a checkout on a platform configured to normalise line endings
#              rewrites each record's single line-feed frame as a carriage return plus line feed.
#              Every record would then be one byte wider than its declared length - a 350-byte
#              archive record would arrive as 352 bytes end to end - and the comparison would
#              fail for a reason that has nothing to do with the code. Marking the fixtures
#              -text pins them to the bytes that were committed, on every platform.
#
#   whitespace Records are space-padded to their declared width, so a record whose trailing field
#              is filler or a short alphanumeric legitimately ends in spaces. Those spaces are
#              content, not a defect: they are what makes the record its contractual width. Left
#              unspecified, `git diff --check` reports each of those lines as a trailing-whitespace
#              error, which buries genuine whitespace defects under thousands of false ones.
#
# The scope is deliberately this directory tree and nothing else. No blanket text=auto or eol
# rule is declared here, because the wrapper script mvnw.cmd is committed with CRLF line endings
# on purpose and a repository-wide normalisation rule would corrupt it.
src/test/resources/fixtures/** -text -whitespace

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Launchers: the line ending is part of the contract, and it is the OPPOSITE for the two wrappers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Three files in this module are executed by an interpreter that reads their first line, and for
# each of them the required ending is a fact about the interpreter rather than a preference. Left
# unspecified, the ending is decided by whatever core.autocrlf the person cloning happens to have
# configured, which is the one input this module cannot see and cannot test:
#
#   mvnw                  A POSIX shell script. With core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout rewrites
#                         all of its lines as CRLF, and the shebang then reads "#!/bin/sh\r" - a
#                         path that does not exist. `./mvnw` fails with "bad interpreter", and so
#                         does the Docker builder stage, which copies this file into a Linux image
#                         and runs it there. eol=lf pins it to LF on every platform.
#
#   mvnw.cmd              A cmd.exe polyglot whose batch half relies on CRLF: cmd.exe reads a
#                         .cmd file line by line, and an LF-only file can leave labels and GOTO
#                         targets unresolved. It was committed as CRLF deliberately; eol=crlf makes
#                         that intent enforceable rather than merely described, so a checkout on a
#                         normalising platform reproduces it instead of depending on a contributor's
#                         configuration. Declaring `text` also normalises the stored blob to LF,
#                         which is what keeps `git diff` and `git diff --check` reading it as text
#                         rather than reporting a carriage return on every line as trailing
#                         whitespace.
#
#   localstack/init/*.sh  A bash hook that is bind-mounted into the LocalStack container and
#                         executed by the Linux shell inside it. The bytes the container reads are
#                         the bytes in the HOST checkout, so a CRLF conversion here breaks the
#                         emulator's startup - "#!/usr/bin/env bash\r" and "set -eu\r" - on a
#                         Windows host even though nothing about the container changed. The CI
#                         bootstrap-contract gate asserts this file is free of carriage returns
#                         after a checkout, and simulates a normalising checkout to prove the
#                         attribute is what holds it.
#
# Anchored with a leading slash where the path is exact, so `mvnw` matches only this module's
# launcher and not a file of the same name at any other depth. Deliberately three narrow rules
# rather than one blanket text=auto: a blanket rule would also claim every fixture above, whose
# bytes are a fixed-width record contract.
/mvnw text eol=lf
/mvnw.cmd text eol=crlf
/localstack/init/*.sh text eol=lf
